  "interpretation": "<p><strong><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">COMMENTS:</span></strong><br><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">Antibodies to SS-A are associated with various Connective Tissue Diseases (CTD). The most common occurrence is in patients </span><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">with Sjogren&rsquo;s syndrome (up to 96% of cases) for which it is considered as a classification criterion. It also reported in SLE, Subacute </span><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">cutaneous Lupus and other CTDs. Antibodies to SS-A can be found in cases of Neonatal Lupus Erythematosus. They are transmitted </span><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">transplacentally to the foetus and cause an inflammatory reaction. While initially this was described to be an SSA type protein with 52 KD </span><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">weight, the recent literature has convincingly demonstrated that it is not associated exclusively with Sjogren&rsquo;s. On the other hand, it </span><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">appears to be more associated with Congenital Heart Block in Neonatal Lupus and with cutaneous lupus manifestations.</span></p>",
